  "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
